#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Apply the completed editorial review for Ezekiel to source content only."""

import json
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def load(path):
    with path.open(encoding="utf-8") as source:
        return json.load(source)


def save(path, value):
    path.write_text(json.dumps(value, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")


WWA = {
    1: "Lamentations closed the exile's grief with an unresolved question rather than a settled answer. Ezekiel opens among the same exiles in Babylon with something different: a priest-prophet's overwhelming vision of God's own throne-chariot, moving freely even far from Jerusalem's temple. Chapter 2 continues directly from this vision, as the voice from the throne commissions Ezekiel to speak to a rebellious people.",
    2: "Chapter 1 ended with Ezekiel facedown before the vision of the LORD's glory. Here that same glory speaks, naming Ezekiel 'son of man' and sending him to a stubborn house of Israel regardless of how they respond, then handing him a scroll filled with lamentation. Chapter 3 continues this commissioning scene as Ezekiel is told to eat the scroll before he can speak it.",
    3: "Chapter 2 ended with a scroll of lamentation held out to Ezekiel. Here he eats it, finds it sweet, and is carried to the exiles at Tel-abib, where God appoints him a watchman responsible for warning both the wicked and the righteous. Chapter 4 turns from this commissioning to Ezekiel's first enacted sign, a model siege of Jerusalem.",
    4: "Chapter 3 commissioned Ezekiel as a watchman responsible to warn. Here he begins acting out that warning instead of merely speaking it: drawing Jerusalem on a clay tile, lying on his side for a measured number of days, and eating siege rations, each detail dramatizing the coming hardship. Chapter 5 continues this sign-act with Ezekiel shaving his head and dividing the hair to picture Jerusalem's threefold fate.",
    5: "Chapter 4 pictured the coming siege through food rationed by weight. Here Ezekiel shaves his head and beard and divides the hair by burning, striking, and scattering it, acting out how Jerusalem's population will be consumed, killed, and scattered because of persistent disobedience. Chapter 6 turns from this citywide sign to a specific oracle against the hilltop shrines across the whole land.",
    6: "Chapter 5 dramatized Jerusalem's coming judgment through divided hair. Here the oracle widens to the mountains of Israel themselves, where the high places and idols that invited judgment will be destroyed along with their worshipers. Chapter 7 presses the same urgency further, announcing that 'the end' has now arrived for the whole land.",
    7: "Chapter 6 condemned the idolatrous shrines on Israel's mountains. Here the message sharpens into urgent, repeated announcements that the end has come: wealth, weapons, trade, and even priests and elders will all fail to avert the disaster. Chapter 8 shifts from this general announcement to a specific vision of exactly what is happening inside Jerusalem's own temple.",
    8: "Chapter 7 announced that the end had come for the whole land. Here a vision carries Ezekiel to Jerusalem's temple itself and shows him the idolatry actually taking place there: an image provoking jealousy, elders burning incense to carved figures, women weeping for Tammuz, and men bowing to the sun. Chapter 9 continues this same temple vision as judgment begins to fall on what Ezekiel has just seen.",
    9: "Chapter 8 showed Ezekiel the hidden idolatry inside Jerusalem's temple. Here judgment begins to fall on it: a man in linen marks those who grieve over the city's sins, while others are sent to strike down everyone left unmarked, starting at the sanctuary itself. Chapter 10 continues this vision as the glory of the LORD begins to leave the temple it has just judged.",
    10: "Chapter 9 ended with judgment carried out inside the temple itself. Here the glory of the LORD begins to depart: coals are scattered from among the cherubim toward the city, and the glory moves from the temple threshold toward the east gate, carried by the same wheeled creatures Ezekiel saw at the Chebar. Chapter 11 continues this departure as the glory leaves the city entirely.",
    11: "Chapter 10 showed the glory of the LORD moving to the temple's east gate. Here it leaves the city altogether, resting on the mountain to the east, even as Ezekiel confronts officials who give destructive counsel and God promises the scattered exiles a new heart and a future gathering. Chapter 12 turns from this vision of departure to Ezekiel acting out the exile that caused it.",
    12: "Chapter 11 promised the scattered exiles a future gathering even as God's glory departed Jerusalem. Here Ezekiel acts out that scattering in advance: packing exile baggage and digging through a wall at dusk to picture the city's ruler fleeing and being captured, while a second sign pictures the anxious, trembling meals of people under siege. Chapter 13 turns to the false prophets who kept promising that none of this would happen.",
    13: "Chapter 12 dramatized the coming exile and dismissed the excuses people used to postpone believing it. Here Ezekiel confronts the false prophets and prophetesses directly, condemning those who announce peace with no word from God and whitewash a crumbling wall instead of repairing it. Chapter 14 turns from public false prophecy to the private idolatry some of Israel's own elders still carry in their hearts.",
    14: "Chapter 13 condemned prophets who offered false reassurance instead of real warning. Here some of Israel's elders come to Ezekiel for guidance while secretly holding idols in their hearts, and God tells them that even Noah, Daniel, and Job could save only themselves, not a nation determined to sin. Chapter 15 turns to a short parable making a similar point about Jerusalem's own uselessness apart from faithfulness.",
    15: "Chapter 14 insisted that not even the most righteous individuals could save a persistently unfaithful nation. Here a short parable compares Jerusalem to the wood of a vine, which is useless for anything but fuel even when whole, and worse once it has already been burned. Chapter 16 expands this same charge of uselessness into the book's longest and most detailed allegory of Jerusalem's unfaithfulness.",
    16: "Chapter 15 compared Jerusalem to worthless, half-burned vine wood. Here a sustained allegory tells Jerusalem's whole history as the story of an abandoned infant whom the LORD rescues, raises, and marries, only to have her repay that care with idolatry and foreign alliances worse than her neighbors Samaria and Sodom. Chapter 17 turns from this personal allegory to a political riddle about Judah's last kings.",
    17: "Chapter 16 ended with a promise that God would remember his covenant despite Jerusalem's unfaithfulness. Here a riddle of two eagles and a cedar retells that same unfaithfulness in political terms: Zedekiah's broken oath to Babylon and his doomed appeal to Egypt, before the chapter closes with a very different planting, a tender shoot God himself sets to grow into a future tree. Chapter 18 turns from this national riddle to a direct dispute over whether individuals suffer for their parents' guilt.",
    18: "Chapter 17 closed with a promise of future hope planted by God's own hand. Here that hope requires a correction first: the exiles' proverb blaming their fathers for their own suffering is rejected, as God insists each person is judged by their own conduct and that even a lifelong sinner who turns can live. Chapter 19 turns from this teaching on individual responsibility to a lament for the fallen royal house as a whole.",
    19: "Chapter 18 insisted that each person answers for their own conduct, not their ancestors'. Here that principle plays out in Judah's own royal history: a lament compares the nation's mother to a lioness whose cubs, her princes, are captured one by one, and then to a once-fruitful vine now uprooted and burned. Chapter 20 turns from this lament to a much longer review of the rebellion that led to this royal collapse.",
    20: "Chapter 19 lamented Judah's royal house as captured lion cubs and a burned vine. Here, when elders come to inquire of him, Ezekiel reviews that same collapse at much greater length, tracing Israel's rebellion back through Egypt and the wilderness even as God's patience, for his own name's sake, kept the nation from being destroyed outright. Chapter 21 turns from this historical review to a sword song naming Jerusalem's judgment directly.",
    21: "Chapter 20 traced Israel's long history of rebellion and God's patient restraint. Here that restraint runs out: a sharpened sword is announced against Jerusalem, Ezekiel acts out mourning gestures, and Babylon's king is pictured choosing by divination which city, Jerusalem or Rabbah, to attack first. Chapter 22 widens the sword's target from Jerusalem's coming siege to a full catalog of the sins that brought it on.",
    22: "Chapter 21 pictured Babylon's king choosing Jerusalem as his target by divination. Here the LORD catalogs exactly why: violence, corrupt leadership, exploitation of the vulnerable, and profaned worship fill the city, pictured as impure metal with no one left willing to stand in the gap to defend it. Chapter 23 returns to allegory to retell this same unfaithfulness through the story of two sisters.",
    23: "Chapter 22 cataloged Jerusalem's sins directly, name by name. Here the same unfaithfulness returns in allegory: Samaria and Jerusalem appear as two sisters, Oholah and Oholibah, whose reckless political and religious entanglements with Assyria and Babylon bring the judgment already announced. Chapter 24 moves from this allegory to the dated moment the judgment actually begins.",
    24: "Chapter 23 pictured Jerusalem's entanglements with foreign powers as a sister's reckless unfaithfulness. Here the judgment those entanglements invited finally arrives: on the very day Babylon's siege begins, Ezekiel compares Jerusalem to a boiling, uncleansable pot, and his own wife's sudden death becomes a sign of the stunned grief still ahead. Chapter 25 turns outward from Jerusalem's own fall to the neighboring nations who will mock it.",
    25: "Chapter 24 marked the day Babylon's siege of Jerusalem began. Here the book turns to Judah's neighbors, Ammon, Moab, Edom, and Philistia, each judged for mocking or exploiting Jerusalem's fall rather than grieving with her. Chapter 26 continues this sequence of oracles against the nations with a much longer judgment on Tyre.",
    26: "Chapter 25 pronounced shorter judgments on four of Judah's neighbors. Here a longer, dated oracle turns to Tyre, condemned for profiting from Jerusalem's ruin and now facing a prolonged siege, ruined walls, and a commercial collapse pictured as a descent into the depths. Chapter 27 continues this same judgment on Tyre with an extended lament over its lost splendor.",
    27: "Chapter 26 announced Tyre's coming destruction and descent into the depths. Here a long lament pictures exactly what will be lost: Tyre as a magnificent merchant ship, built and supplied by nations across the known world, suddenly wrecked while its trading partners mourn from the shore. Chapter 28 turns from Tyre's commercial fall to the personal pride of its ruler.",
    28: "Chapter 27 lamented Tyre's wrecked trading network. Here the oracle turns to Tyre's own ruler, condemned for claiming to be a god despite being mortal, and to its king, pictured in Edenic, cherub-like splendor before his fall, followed by a brief judgment on neighboring Sidon and a promise that Israel will finally dwell securely. Chapter 29 turns from these Phoenician cities to Egypt, the last and largest nation addressed in this sequence.",
    29: "Chapter 28 closed its Phoenician oracles with a promise of secure dwelling for Israel. Here the oracles against the nations turn to Egypt: Pharaoh is pictured as a great river-creature claiming the Nile as his own, judged for failing Israel as a reliable ally, though a limited restoration is promised after forty years. Chapter 30 continues this judgment on Egypt, naming the day of the LORD that will fall on it and its allies.",
    30: "Chapter 29 pictured Pharaoh as a boastful river-creature about to be judged. Here that judgment is named outright as a day of the LORD: Egypt's cities, idols, and military strength will all fail together, with Pharaoh's own arm broken even as Babylon's arm grows strong. Chapter 31 continues this same Egyptian judgment through the image of a once-towering cedar cut down.",
    31: "Chapter 30 announced a day of the LORD breaking Pharaoh's strength. Here Egypt is warned through the example of Assyria, pictured as an unmatched cedar watered by deep streams and admired by every nation, whose very splendor led to pride, felling, and a descent among the dead. Chapter 32 closes this long sequence of Egyptian oracles with two final laments for Pharaoh himself.",
    32: "Chapter 31 warned Egypt through Assyria's own fall from towering cedar to felled corpse. Here two laments complete the sequence: Pharaoh is pictured as a sea creature finally caught and cast aside, and then as one more fallen power joining Assyria, Elam, and others already lying in the realm of the dead. Chapter 33 turns from these oracles against foreign nations back to Ezekiel's own responsibility as a watchman to his people.",
    33: "Chapter 32 closed the long sequence of oracles against the nations with Egypt's descent among the already-fallen. Here the book turns back to Judah itself: Ezekiel's watchman role is renewed, a fugitive finally arrives with news that Jerusalem has fallen, and Ezekiel challenges survivors who claim the land by right and exiles who enjoy his words without ever acting on them. Chapter 34 turns from these failed hearers to the failed leaders, Israel's own shepherds, who let this happen.",
    34: "Chapter 33 showed exiles who heard Ezekiel's words without doing them. Here their leaders come under the same kind of scrutiny: Israel's shepherds are condemned for feeding themselves instead of the flock, and God promises to seek out the scattered sheep himself and set 'my servant David' over them as one true shepherd under a covenant of peace. Chapter 35 turns from this promise for Israel to a judgment on the neighbor who gloated over its scattering.",
    35: "Chapter 34 promised Israel a true shepherd after its own leaders' failure. Here that promise finds its necessary counterpart in judgment on Edom, condemned under the name Mount Seir for its lasting hostility and for claiming Israel's desolated territory as its own. Chapter 36 turns back to that same territory, Israel's own mountains, now promised restoration rather than loss.",
    36: "Chapter 35 condemned Edom for claiming Israel's desolated land. Here Israel's own mountains receive the opposite promise: renewed fertility and resettlement, pursued not for Israel's own merit but for the sake of God's name, culminating in the promise of a new heart and a new spirit that will finally make obedience possible. Chapter 37 pictures that same renewal even more dramatically, as a valley of dry bones comes back to life.",
    37: "Chapter 36 promised a new heart and spirit for a renewed Israel. Here that same renewal is pictured as dead bones brought back to life in a valley, a sign of hope for the whole house of Israel, followed by two sticks joined into one to show Judah and Joseph reunited under a single Davidic shepherd and an everlasting covenant. Chapter 38 turns from this picture of secure, reunited peace to one final, large-scale threat against it.",
    38: "Chapter 37 pictured Israel reunited and secure under one shepherd and an everlasting covenant. Here that very security draws a massive final threat: Gog of Magog, leading a coalition of distant peoples, is summoned against restored Israel, though the chapter already announces his defeat as proof of God's holiness before the nations. Chapter 39 completes this same battle, describing Gog's actual destruction and its lingering aftermath.",
    39: "Chapter 38 announced Gog's coming defeat before restored Israel even faces him. Here that defeat is carried out in full: Gog's forces are destroyed, their weapons burned for fuel, and their dead buried over many months, after which God promises to stop hiding his face from Israel and to pour out his Spirit on the people instead. Chapter 40 leaves this battle behind for an entirely different kind of vision: a new temple, measured in careful detail.",
    40: "Chapter 39 ended with God promising to pour out his Spirit on a restored Israel. Here, fourteen years after Jerusalem's fall, Ezekiel is carried in vision to a high mountain and shown a new temple complex, as a guide with a measuring reed surveys its outer walls, gates, and courts in careful detail. Chapter 41 continues this same measuring tour into the temple building itself.",
    41: "Chapter 40 measured the new temple's outer walls, gates, and courts. Here the guide moves inward, measuring the sanctuary, its inner room, and the surrounding chambers, now decorated with carved cherubim and palm trees like Solomon's temple before it. Chapter 42 continues the tour outward again to the priests' chambers and the precinct's outer wall.",
    42: "Chapter 41 measured the temple building's sanctuary and inner decoration. Here the guide shows Ezekiel the priests' chambers, where holy offerings are eaten and garments are changed, and measures the entire precinct, marked off by a wall separating holy space from common. Chapter 43 brings this long measuring tour to its purpose, as the glory Ezekiel once saw departing now returns to fill this temple.",
    43: "Chapter 42 finished measuring the new temple's precinct and its boundary wall. Here the reason for all that careful measurement arrives: the glory of Israel's God returns from the east and fills the temple, and Ezekiel is told that the people must reject the very defilement that drove that glory away the first time. Chapter 44 turns from this return of glory to the rules now governing who may approach it.",
    44: "Chapter 43 described the glory of God returning to fill the new temple. Here access to that glory is carefully regulated: the east gate it entered by stays shut, Levites who strayed into idolatry lose their place at the altar, and only the sons of Zadok are allowed to serve there, under detailed rules for dress, teaching, and purity. Chapter 45 turns from priestly regulation to the land itself, allotted around this same temple.",
    45: "Chapter 44 regulated who could serve at the new temple's altar. Here the land around that temple is apportioned among sanctuary, priests, Levites, city, and prince, with rulers specifically ordered to reject violence and use honest measures. Chapter 46 continues these regulations with the prince's own worship and offerings.",
    46: "Chapter 45 apportioned land around the new temple and demanded honest measures from its rulers. Here further rules describe the prince's own worship through a designated gate, the rhythm of sabbath and new-moon offerings, and kitchens built for preparing the people's sacrifices, alongside a rule preventing the prince from permanently seizing anyone's inherited land. Chapter 47 turns from these worship regulations to a vision of life flowing out from the temple itself.",
    47: "Chapter 46 protected ordinary people's land from royal seizure. Here life flows outward instead: water from the temple threshold deepens into a river that heals the Dead Sea itself, lined with trees whose fruit and leaves sustain and heal, before the chapter turns to mark out the land's boundaries and include resident foreigners in its inheritance. Chapter 48 completes this final vision with the actual division of that land among the tribes.",
    48: "Chapter 47 extended the land's inheritance even to resident foreigners. Here the book's last vision divides that same land among Israel's twelve tribes, sets apart the sanctuary and the city with its twelve gates, and closes with the city's new name: 'The LORD is there.' The next book, Daniel, follows a different exiled prophet through the same Babylonian captivity, from the court of Nebuchadnezzar to visions of kingdoms still to come.",
}

